	In article <1991Feb24.012929.8905@agate.berkeley.edu>, Tom Holub
(tom@monsoon.Berkeley.edu) writes:

>The concept is, if you use a charge off a wand, the storekeeper wants
>to charge you for it.  Fine.

>If you buy a wand, the storekeeper wants to charge you for it.  Fine.
>If you use a charge off a wand, and *then* want to buy it, he charges
>you for *both*.

>This could be right.  I mean, if you buy a handgun, and you fire one
>round to test it, the store owner could concievably charge you for
>the gun, the 5 bullets in the gun, and the bullet you fired.

	Far be it from be to belabor the point, but the analogy isn't
quite right, since the wand is not restored to its original number of 
charges.  You don't get the charge back when you buy it.
	A better analogy would be the following:  When you buy a handgun,
and fire a round to test it, it would be fair for the shopkeeper to 
charge you the price of a gun and five bullets, even though you're only
walking out of the store with a gun and four bullets.  It would _not_ be
fair to be charged the price of the one bullet you fired, and the price
of a gun with five bullets in it.

